The error interface is one method. A struct that adds it becomes an error, and its fields travel with the failure:

```go
type NotFoundError struct {
    Name string
}

func (e *NotFoundError) Error() string {
    return "not found: " + e.Name
}

// returning it
func lookup(name string) error {
    return &NotFoundError{Name: name}
}
```

The caller's side is errors.As — the type-aware chain walk:

```go
err := lookup("cfg")
var nfe *NotFoundError
if errors.As(err, &nfe) {
    fmt.Println("which one:", nfe.Name)   // the field the caller needed
}
```

That's the whole pattern: struct for the data, Error() for the message, pointer receiver for the method, errors.As at the consumer. The value over a plain errors.New string is exactly the fields — 'which record failed' stops being prose in a message and becomes data the caller branches on.

The sentinel (errors.New) gives the caller one bit: is it this error? The type gives the caller the error AND its specifics:

```go
// sentinel — the caller learns WHICH failure, nothing else
var ErrRateLimited = errors.New("rate limited")

// type — the caller learns which failure and HOW LONG to wait
type RateLimitError struct{ RetryAfter time.Duration }
```

The caller's next action is the whole point. With the sentinel, the caller can branch — 'if rate limited, use the backup' — but not act on specifics. With the type, the caller reads the fields and does something with them: sleep the RetryAfter, show the UserMessage, redirect to the Location, log the Which-Record.

When types become mandatory: the moment the caller's correct behavior depends on data about the failure, not just the category. Retry logic needs a duration. HTTP error responses need a status code. Partial-failure reporting needs which shard. String errors can't carry any of this — parsing the message is the forbidden alternative, because it couples the caller to your prose.

The progression in real codebases: start with errors.New sentinels, promote to a custom type when a caller first needs to ask a question the message can't answer structurally. The standard library made the same journey — os.PathError exists because 'which path?' is the first question every file-error caller asks.
